WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.500
- [Narrator] It seems like every year,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.180
there's a real threat of a...

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.013
- [Reporter 1] Government shutdown

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:05.250
- [Reporter 2] Lawmakers
racing to reach a deal

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.720
to avoid a government shutdown.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.730
- [Narrator] And 2023 is no different.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.850
- Saturday, 11:59 PM, deadline to avoid

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.170
a government shutdown.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.100
- [Narrator] Here's why the government

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.280
is constantly on the verge of a shutdown.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.650
For starters, the US is the only country

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.345
that even can shut down.

00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:23.490
Everywhere else, if a government agency

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.800
doesn't have a budget
for the new fiscal year,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.590
they just use last
year's and keep working.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.720
But because 150 years ago,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.640
a bunch of US agencies would blow

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.050
through their entire budget

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.510
or appropriations in a few months,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.450
Congress passed the Anti-Deficiency Act,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.240
saying it wasn't lawful to spend any sum

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.820
in excess of appropriations
made by Congress

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.590
for that fiscal year.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.270
Basically, if Congress doesn't pass

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.040
a new funding bill every year,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.730
agencies can't spend a dime and shut down.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.593
But why that keeps threatening to happen

00:00:55.593 --> 00:00:59.040
a century later comes down to Congress?

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.460
For one, these spending bills
are kind of their big thing.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.140
- The one thing that Congress
has to do with the president

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.980
is write the bills to keep the
government open and working.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.630
There are a lot of measures

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.870
that should be done or could be done,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.126
but this is the be-all,
end-all of must pass.

00:01:19.126 --> 00:01:21.270
- [Narrator] So these
spending bills end up becoming

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.470
a vessel for all kinds of
other things like last year's.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.610
Sure, it funded things

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.680
like the Department of
Agriculture and Energy

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.240
and the Department of Defense,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.080
but it also created the Alaska
Salmon Research Task Force,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.510
renamed a bunch of post offices,

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.820
and changed policy on
the protection of whales

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.140
in the North Atlantic,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.560
which environmental activists
were adamantly opposed to.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.180
- Other things are gonna catch a ride,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.120
and that's sort of the
reality of living in a world

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.550
where Congress struggles to legislate.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.200
- [Narrator] She isn't exaggerating.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.430
This is the number of laws

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.130
that each session of Congress has enacted.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.030
Not only is Congress trending
towards fewer and fewer laws,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.900
but so far this session, they're
off to a really slow start,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.360
they're on pace to not
even create a hundred.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.990
- If there's so few things
that make it to the floor,

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.980
it's the target, it's your
opportunity to make a stand.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.920
It makes it harder and harder
to reach those agreements

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.390
in the first place.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.040
- [Narrator] In theory,
they should be reaching

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.600
those agreements with something called

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.600
a budget resolution by about mid-April,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.330
but that hasn't happened in 20 years.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.310
Then they're supposed to
pass those spending bills

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.130
by the new fiscal year
deadline, October 1st.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.950
Here's how many spending bills
have been passed by then.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.450
It's been 26 years since all
of them were done on time.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.360
They usually pass a short-term
stopgap funding bill

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.220
or a continuing resolution

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.770
to fund the government
for just a little bit.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.690
- And that's why it's seemingly

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.110
that we come back in
December quite frequently,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.810
and that's when the rubber hits the road

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.630
for actually compiling
all these final decisions

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.220
on spending bills.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.130
- [Narrator] The ideological
divide in Congress is growing.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.230
Republicans are more conservative,

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.980
Democrats are more liberal,
and then there's few members

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.630
who meet somewhere in the middle.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.500
- Polarization exacerbates
difficulties in the process here.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.810
- [Narrator] You can see
it in split districts too

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.450
where voters elected a
representative from one party

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.003
and voted the opposite
party for president.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.210
Today, Congress is one
of the fewest numbers

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.760
of split districts in the last 100 years.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.590
- It reinforces partisanship,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.070
it makes it harder to
reach final agreements

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.290
where you have to reach
across the party line,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.150
and it creates an incentive for speakers

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.805
like McCarthy never to wanna
deal with the Democrats,

00:03:39.805 --> 00:03:42.930
but to deliver on behalf
of his party's conference,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.919
not on behalf of what the Democrats want.

00:03:44.919 --> 00:03:46.650
(suspenseful music)

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.450
- [Narrator] So a
disorganized budget process,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.910
plus a lack of opportunity
to debate things,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.090
plus a lack of motivation to
compromise on those things,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.780
means funding the government
is unsurprisingly a battle.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.100
- Look, I'm working everything we can

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.777
to make sure this doesn't happen.

00:04:00.777 --> 00:04:03.777
(suspenseful music)

